# No Push / Remote Sync Without Approval

Do **not** push, sync, or otherwise update a remote repository or branch unless the user has explicitly approved it in the current conversation.

## Requires approval before running

- `git push` (any form, including `-u`, `--force-with-lease`, tags)
- `git push --delete` / remote branch deletion
- Sync actions that update the remote (e.g. IDE “Sync”, “Publish Branch”, push-after-commit)
- Any other command that uploads commits, tags, or refs to `origin` (or another remote)

## Allowed without extra approval

- Local-only git operations: `status`, `diff`, `log`, `add`, `commit` (still follow the separate “only commit when asked” rule)
- `git fetch` / `git pull` only when needed to inspect or update the local view — still ask first if the user has not asked you to pull/rebase/merge from remote

## How to request approval

Before any remote update:

1. Briefly state what you would push/sync (branch name, remote, and whether it is first publish or new commits).
2. Ask for explicit approval (e.g. “Push `Issue/N/slug` to `origin`?”).
3. Wait for a clear yes (e.g. “push”, “yes”, “go ahead”) before running the command.

## Examples

```text
❌ BAD — push without asking
git push -u origin HEAD

✅ GOOD — ask first, then push only after approval
"Ready to push Issue/12/csv-ingest to origin. Should I push?"
# wait for user: "yes" / "push it"
git push -u origin HEAD
```

If unclear whether the user approved a push, ask again — do not push.
